Task 1: Install and Configure 2FA Service
Subtask 1.1: Verify IdM Server Status
First, let's ensure our IdM server is running properly and check the current authentication methods.

# Check IdM server status
sudo systemctl status ipa

# Verify IdM services are running
sudo ipactl status

# Check current authentication methods
kinit admin
ipa config-show | grep -i auth
Subtask 1.2: Install Required 2FA Packages
Install the necessary packages for OTP (One-Time Password) support in IdM.

# Install OTP-related packages
sudo dnf install -y freeipa-server-trust-ad python3-qrcode

# Verify installation
rpm -qa | grep -i otp
Subtask 1.3: Enable OTP Support in IdM
Configure IdM to support OTP tokens for two-factor authentication.

# Authenticate as admin
kinit admin

# Check if OTP is already enabled
ipa otpconfig-show

# If not configured, set up basic OTP configuration
ipa otpconfig-mod --hotp-window=10 --totp-window=5
Subtask 1.4: Configure RADIUS Support (Optional)
For enhanced 2FA capabilities, configure RADIUS support.

# Install FreeRADIUS packages
sudo dnf install -y freeradius freeradius-utils

# Configure basic RADIUS settings
sudo systemctl enable radiusd
sudo systemctl start radiusd

# Verify RADIUS is running
sudo systemctl status radiusd
Task 2: Enforce 2FA Policies for Users
Subtask 2.1: Create Test Users
Create test users to demonstrate 2FA functionality.

# Create test users
ipa user-add testuser1 --first=Test --last=User1 --password

ipa user-add testuser2 --first=Test --last=User2 --password

# Verify users were created
ipa user-find test*
Subtask 2.2: Configure Global 2FA Policy
Set up global authentication policies requiring 2FA.

# Create a new authentication indicator policy
ipa pwpolicy-add global_2fa_policy --maxlife=90 --minlife=1

# View current password policies
ipa pwpolicy-find
Subtask 2.3: Create User Authentication Types
Configure different authentication types for users.

# Set user authentication type to require OTP
ipa user-mod testuser1 --user-auth-type=otp

# Set user authentication type to require both password and OTP
ipa user-mod testuser2 --user-auth-type=password --user-auth-type=otp

# Verify authentication types
ipa user-show testuser1 | grep -i auth
ipa user-show testuser2 | grep -i auth
Subtask 2.4: Create Host-Based Authentication Policy
Configure host-based authentication policies for enhanced security.

# Create HBAC rule for 2FA users
ipa hbacrule-add secure_hosts_2fa

# Add users to the rule
ipa hbacrule-add-user secure_hosts_2fa --users=testuser1,testuser2

# Add hosts to the rule (use your current hostname)
HOSTNAME=$(hostname)
ipa hbacrule-add-host secure_hosts_2fa --hosts=$HOSTNAME

# Add services to the rule
ipa hbacrule-add-service secure_hosts_2fa --hbacsvcs=sshd

# Verify the rule
ipa hbacrule-show secure_hosts_2fa
Task 3: Test the 2FA Authentication Process
Subtask 3.1: Generate OTP Tokens for Users
Create OTP tokens that users can add to their authenticator apps.

# Generate OTP token for testuser1
ipa otptoken-add --owner=testuser1 --desc="TestUser1 Mobile Token"

# The command will output a QR code and URI - save this information
# Example output will show: URI and QR code for scanning

# Generate OTP token for testuser2
ipa otptoken-add --owner=testuser2 --desc="TestUser2 Mobile Token"
Subtask 3.2: Set Up Mobile Authenticator App Simulation
Since we're in a lab environment, we'll simulate the mobile app using command-line tools.

# Install oath-toolkit for generating TOTP codes
sudo dnf install -y oathtool

# Extract the secret key from the OTP token (you'll need the actual secret from the previous step)
# This is typically shown in the URI as secret=XXXXX

# Example of generating TOTP code (replace SECRET_KEY with actual secret)
# oathtool --totp -b SECRET_KEY

# List OTP tokens to get details
ipa otptoken-find --owner=testuser1
Subtask 3.3: Test SSH Authentication with 2FA
Test the 2FA authentication process using SSH.

# First, ensure SSH is configured for 2FA
sudo cp /etc/ssh/sshd_config /etc/ssh/sshd_config.backup

# Configure SSH for 2FA
sudo tee -a /etc/ssh/sshd_config << EOF

# 2FA Configuration
ChallengeResponseAuthentication yes
AuthenticationMethods publickey,keyboard-interactive:pam
UsePAM yes
EOF

# Restart SSH service
sudo systemctl restart sshd

# Test authentication from another terminal or session
# ssh testuser1@localhost
# You will be prompted for both password and OTP code
Subtask 3.4: Test Web UI Authentication
Test 2FA through the IdM web interface.

# Get the IdM server URL
echo "IdM Web UI URL: https://$(hostname)/ipa/ui/"

# Open web browser and navigate to the URL
# Login with testuser1 credentials
# You should be prompted for OTP code after password
